#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059409 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b60a8ae-c04f-530a-b89c-f8ac70969d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Conditional Statements as Disjunctions"}] :question/id #uuid "48364248-d296-502c-96da-d1905be35627" :question/math-academy-id "e-12707" :question/problem "Which statement is logically equivalent to the following conditional statement?\n\n$\\qquad$ If you take an umbrella, then you will stay dry." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A{:}$ You take an umbrella.\n\n- $B{:}$ You will stay dry.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A \\Rightarrow B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\neg A \\lor B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $\\neg A{:}$ You do not take an umbrella.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ You do not take an umbrella, or you will stay dry.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ Either you do not take an umbrella, or you will stay dry."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059410 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eec6cf11-74cc-5b3a-b2fe-bd4285fe4a88" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Conditional Statement Using the Associative, Commutative, and Absorption Laws"}] :question/id #uuid "caa49bb8-c66f-56cb-a3d2-a7616abee38c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12708" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $\\neg (A \\Rightarrow \\neg B) \\lor A.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the conditional statement $A \\Rightarrow \\neg B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $\\lnot A \\lor \\neg B.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\neg (A \\Rightarrow \\neg B) \\lor A \\equiv \\neg (\\neg A \\lor \\neg B) \\lor A.\n$$\n\nNow, we can distribute the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law:\n\n$$\n\\neg (\\neg A \\lor \\neg B) \\lor A \\equiv ( \\neg (\\neg A) \\land \\neg (\\neg B) ) \\lor A\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n( \\neg (\\neg A) \\land \\neg (\\neg B) ) \\lor A \\equiv (A \\land B) \\lor A.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the absorption law, we have\n\n$$\n(A \\land B) \\lor A \\equiv A.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059411 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/id #uuid "b9a65dc8-528a-5c3c-b518-a2bbecc551c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-12709" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y \\equiv \\neg (X \\land Y)$\n\n- $\\neg \\left(X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\right)\\equiv \\neg X \\land Y$\n\n- $\\neg \\left(X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\right)\\equiv X \\land Y$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $\\neg X \\lor \\neg Y,$ which by De Morgan's law is equivalent to $\\neg (X\\land Y).$\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement II is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nX \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\equiv \\neg (X\\land Y)\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n\\neg \\left(X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\right) \\equiv \\neg \\left(\\neg (X\\land Y)\\right).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nX\\land Y.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\neg \\left(X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\right)$ is equivalent to $X \\land Y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059425 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0936217d-66d2-5360-b88b-a30683720415" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Disjunctive Statements as Conditionals"}] :question/id #uuid "54512495-0ca8-55a3-834d-0b70f96a5fe7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13231" :question/problem "Which conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement below?\n\n$\\qquad$ Use sunscreen, or you will get sunburn." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A{:}$ Use sunscreen.\n\n- $B{:}$ You will get sunburn.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A \\lor B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\neg A \\Rightarrow B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $\\neg A{:}$ You don't use sunscreen.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ If you don't use sunscreen, then you will get sunburn."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eec6cf11-74cc-5b3a-b2fe-bd4285fe4a88" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Conditional Statement Using the Associative, Commutative, and Absorption Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "425436d7-4f12-57df-915c-487746dbd6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A∨B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572904} {:answer/id #uuid "751c4258-966c-5529-941d-de9c9266930c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬A∧¬B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572972} {:answer/id #uuid "785c00c3-47f5-5e21-bd85-d44b5a2ca5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬A∨B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572979} {:answer/id #uuid "c11456cc-728e-58e8-949d-a75492351b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573068} {:answer/id #uuid "deb962e3-f3bf-5667-9728-d3ff4fdfa513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬A∨¬B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "deb962e3-f3bf-5667-9728-d3ff4fdfa513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬A∨¬B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573110} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd4d4d26-9774-5101-8a1e-2da3a880ab36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d17f8721-9970-54a5-a0a5-023397a542c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-102734" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬(¬A∨¬B)⇒¬A.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(¬A∨¬B)⇒¬A≡¬(¬(¬A∨¬B))∨¬A.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(¬A∨¬B))∨¬A≡(¬A∨¬B)∨¬A.\n$$\n\nUsing the commutative and associative law, we have\n\n$$\n(¬A∨¬B)∨¬A≡(¬A∨¬A)∨¬B.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the idempotent law, we have\n\n$$\n(¬A∨¬A)∨¬B≡¬A∨¬B.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $¬A∨¬B.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98d0148c-06ef-5117-bace-03063dd6360d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A∨B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378714} {:answer/id #uuid "f9df4014-2df2-5124-bf2b-638e928d7002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬B∨A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378715} {:answer/id #uuid "39a579bf-496b-548c-bd9f-6607183041f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬B∧A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378716} {:answer/id #uuid "c70b7f6e-bb66-5469-8638-e480696d51ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬A∧B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378717} {:answer/id #uuid "469bc761-0a98-5c2a-9f58-31c271c74501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A∨¬B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98d0148c-06ef-5117-bace-03063dd6360d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A∨B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378714} :answer-field/id #uuid "bef1e854-dcf0-5bf2-bce1-3c3b1e51137e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe611b4a-33af-5b80-a6da-077998756db6" :question/math-academy-id "q-1469" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬A⇒B.$" :question/worked-solution "The conditional statement $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q:$\n\nSo, in this case, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\n¬A⇒B≡¬(¬A)∨B\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬A)∨B≡A∨B.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $A∨B.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b60a8ae-c04f-530a-b89c-f8ac70969d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Conditional Statements as Disjunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ab520b5-59f6-5feb-953d-189ae99c37f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Study, and you won't pass the exam." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411018} {:answer/id #uuid "9dcbcf84-c1f5-5b0f-adcd-61e18ef3dcfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Study, and you will pass the exam." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411028} {:answer/id #uuid "9f052a76-d5ae-5be9-8f72-62412156c92a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Study, or you won't pass the exam." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411029} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ec8d87-b0ad-57ac-b03e-07c4c8ad6b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Pass the exam, or study." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411030} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f8fb5e-a233-53f5-8f1c-029c69f30db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Don't study, and you won't pass the exam." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f052a76-d5ae-5be9-8f72-62412156c92a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Study, or you won't pass the exam." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411029} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cd696de-2baa-518f-9a46-b4c3a6f28345" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a21e616e-14f9-5690-b744-7b4501640855" :question/math-academy-id "q-182455" :question/problem "$\\,$ If you don’t study, then you won't pass the exam.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You don't study.\n\n- $B:$ You won't pass the exam.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You study.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You study, or you won't pass the exam.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Study, or you won't pass the exam."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c8d5ea2-5c8e-5863-b634-8172a1da8c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬A∧¬B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057219} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9b69c3-6af3-523b-a894-d20f75430af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A∧¬B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057220} {:answer/id #uuid "4aa812d0-26e4-513f-bf92-5131225f1ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t$ (tautology)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057221} {:answer/id #uuid "5238cd11-e5bb-5305-b6e9-b0b21b2429d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A∨B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057222} {:answer/id #uuid "692cf23e-315d-592d-8ca9-2929fd245e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬A∧B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab9b69c3-6af3-523b-a894-d20f75430af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A∧¬B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057220} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d3318c5-0318-518c-b129-a1623899826b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "199ef1bf-29a7-5792-bf23-b4a22e87950a" :question/math-academy-id "q-182469" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬(A⇒B).$" :question/worked-solution "The conditional statement $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q:$\n\nSo, in this case, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\n¬(A⇒B)≡¬(¬A∨B)\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬A∨B)≡¬(¬A)∧¬B.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nA∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $A∧¬B.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54794834-147f-5ca2-b3aa-a53057dc42b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬(M∨N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411012} {:answer/id #uuid "67bc61ce-a9ad-55ef-865b-df1dad087605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬M∨N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411016} {:answer/id #uuid "912c3b4c-072c-58f6-b64b-c9f5e02a6ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬(M∧N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411026} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a5b11e-6587-5ea7-9a34-acdcb5a0d32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∧¬N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411037} {:answer/id #uuid "c15a47c8-499b-599d-8986-58576b3db58a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∧N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "912c3b4c-072c-58f6-b64b-c9f5e02a6ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬(M∧N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411026} :answer-field/id #uuid "863e1035-b7f5-5d5b-b991-ac77fd6a7db6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17e8ba9f-418a-585c-9332-bdf5c1ee589a" :question/math-academy-id "q-182493" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $M⇒¬N.$" :question/worked-solution "The conditional statement $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q:$\n\nSo, in this case, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nM⇒¬N≡¬M∨¬N\n$$\n\nUsing De Morgan's law, we reach\n\n$$\n¬M∨¬N≡¬(M∧N).\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $¬(M∧N).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bb25c3e-15c7-56b1-961b-3c21dd8e7b57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411013} {:answer/id #uuid "7bffc6ae-a7c2-591c-bc55-a77e46655d6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∨¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411019} {:answer/id #uuid "9233ceaf-9f1c-5208-b9cd-674ecda2cce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬X∨Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411027} {:answer/id #uuid "b621cd70-8e5a-56ee-b272-20c6098bad66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬X∧¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411035} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b04d6d-0c18-5c36-91f6-4f598bc216d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bb25c3e-15c7-56b1-961b-3c21dd8e7b57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411013} :answer-field/id #uuid "b19ad54e-7fbb-5207-bf8a-3ab93b802ddf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb0a268b-ca46-5b91-9359-0e418e71bab9" :question/math-academy-id "q-182494" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬(X⇒Y).$" :question/worked-solution "The conditional statement $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q:$\n\nSo, in this case, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\n¬(X⇒Y)≡¬(¬X∨Y)\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬X∨Y)≡¬(¬X)∧¬Y.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nX∧¬Y.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $X∧¬Y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "103ba745-89da-572a-8645-0e48e298eb69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453628} {:answer/id #uuid "6db316e3-11cf-581e-aba4-72198d752449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "103ba745-89da-572a-8645-0e48e298eb69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453628} :answer-field/id #uuid "08fbb3c2-9e09-5166-81cf-b13e5f40f715" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454564} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8908055-e767-5cc9-8603-c9755f6acf08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453630} {:answer/id #uuid "2dee54e9-a7cf-5ff7-8cb7-66dd7d69200c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8908055-e767-5cc9-8603-c9755f6acf08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453630} :answer-field/id #uuid "605e62e8-9662-50d1-bfd8-1829ceb621ed" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454565} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7adbcc1-3421-5d01-aa06-a9b6e0ef1051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453632} {:answer/id #uuid "502be7d4-5665-556d-b837-f469b7e58366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "502be7d4-5665-556d-b837-f469b7e58366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453633} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3efa2ca-31f9-5aba-80a2-9f4d61b15725" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b9dc8cb9-65d8-59f4-a25c-a9f9b514997d" :question/math-academy-id "q-182505" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $¬P⇒Q≡P∨¬Q$\n- **II.** $¬(¬P⇒Q)≡¬P∧Q$\n- **III.** $¬(¬P⇒Q)≡¬P∧¬Q$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $¬P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬(¬P)∨Q,$ which in turn is equivalent to $P∨Q.$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\n¬P⇒Q≡P∨Q\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(¬P⇒Q)≡¬(P∨Q).\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(P∨Q)≡¬P∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(¬P⇒Q)$ is equivalent to $¬P∧¬Q.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b60a8ae-c04f-530a-b89c-f8ac70969d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Conditional Statements as Disjunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "031baea5-6ff9-513e-b2ec-02a90e898fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you follow the recipe, or the dish will be delicious." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410998} {:answer/id #uuid "087177ce-72c4-5ef2-938f-b10e9e8b40fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Follow the recipe, and the dish won't be delicious." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411001} {:answer/id #uuid "0fbae154-dca8-5832-b6f3-6ca25402e83c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you do not follow the recipe, or the dish will be delicious." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411003} {:answer/id #uuid "4c22c6ff-4399-5df2-b80e-59ddc05f68af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you follow the recipe, or the dish won't be delicious." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411009} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9f9df7-1486-526b-82e1-407e863e4309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Don't follow the recipe, or the dish won't be delicious." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fbae154-dca8-5832-b6f3-6ca25402e83c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you do not follow the recipe, or the dish will be delicious." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411003} :answer-field/id #uuid "9134464d-6e44-5f65-9ae5-628575a51af6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "879f4dd5-148a-578f-a2fe-b0846abdf946" :question/math-academy-id "q-182509" :question/problem "$\\,$ If you follow the recipe, the dish will be delicious.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You follow the recipe.\n\n- $B:$ The dish will be delicious.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You do not follow the recipe.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You do not follow the recipe, or the dish will be delicious.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Either you do not follow the recipe, or the dish will be delicious.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b60a8ae-c04f-530a-b89c-f8ac70969d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Conditional Statements as Disjunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daafb725-18a1-5b1f-bbf2-b7948d5133da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Water the plants, or they will die." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379190} {:answer/id #uuid "ca421a7e-2675-5c32-89ae-a9fda006aa2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you do not water the plants, or they won't die." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379191} {:answer/id #uuid "8f121c31-53ac-5e3c-b988-3ebd1a177aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you do not water the plants, or they will die." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379192} {:answer/id #uuid "c84e1f0d-e8de-5896-b55a-24dc484421d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Don't water the plants, and they won't die." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379193} {:answer/id #uuid "f70cd024-5b78-594f-b2cb-313b1e2fdd75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Water the plants, or they won't die." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daafb725-18a1-5b1f-bbf2-b7948d5133da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Water the plants, or they will die." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379190} :answer-field/id #uuid "f07f5bdb-e227-5da5-a1ad-9a06210248d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8e2e4f2-2d18-5c88-8196-4118be9f7f32" :question/math-academy-id "q-182512" :question/problem "$\\,$ If you don't water the plants, then they will die.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You don't water the plants.\n\n- $B:$ The plants will die.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You water the plants.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You water the plants, or the plants will die.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Water the plants, or they will die."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b60a8ae-c04f-530a-b89c-f8ac70969d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Conditional Statements as Disjunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a11ca8b-4db9-5745-913c-b7d2c822948d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Don't submit your assignment, and you won't get a zero." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572851} {:answer/id #uuid "62f6689c-e1aa-59c6-969a-a053017be4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you do not submit your assignment, or you won't get a zero." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572941} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa409c9-3b6c-521c-8018-31c3aa75ce9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Submit your assignment, or you won't get a zero." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572961} {:answer/id #uuid "a2513616-2a1d-50a1-8859-18c121631f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Submit your assignment, or you will get a zero." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573031} {:answer/id #uuid "fabc6d0a-ca89-586c-90d6-3e44b1de752e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you do not submit your assignment, or you will get a zero." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2513616-2a1d-50a1-8859-18c121631f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Submit your assignment, or you will get a zero." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573031} :answer-field/id #uuid "6243668f-f17a-5b03-913e-72b8f476068f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ffccd8c-fd28-5be4-b193-a3d833fff80a" :question/math-academy-id "q-182513" :question/problem "$\\,$ If you don't submit your assignment, then you will get a zero.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You don't submit your assignment.\n\n- $B:$ You will get a zero.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You submit your assignment.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You submit your assignment, or you will get a zero.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Submit your assignment, or you will get a zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b60a8ae-c04f-530a-b89c-f8ac70969d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Conditional Statements as Disjunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "309deb5e-b30d-502d-b896-e1f163bb596b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Turn the volume up, and you won't hear what they're saying." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572877} {:answer/id #uuid "8529207d-33ec-5310-8f93-ac4dc4020cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Hear what they're saying, or turn the volume up." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572995} {:answer/id #uuid "a936808b-8124-518b-b459-f435422af3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Turn the volume up, or you won't hear what they're saying." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573040} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1701d1-8ce6-5bed-8a35-b8cf1174656f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Turn the volume up, and you will hear what they're saying." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573079} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2fdd88-5b07-5cc9-9f2d-69efb801b26a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Don't turn the volume up, and you won't hear what they're saying." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a936808b-8124-518b-b459-f435422af3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Turn the volume up, or you won't hear what they're saying." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573040} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d4b159f-3c60-5c4b-a838-942cc712f24e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf7bb594-de49-54f5-b420-2e5186a68020" :question/math-academy-id "q-182569" :question/problem "$\\,$ If you don't turn the volume up, then you won't hear what they're saying.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You don't turn the volume up.\n\n- $B:$ You won't hear what they're saying.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You turn the volume up.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You turn the volume up, or you won't hear what they're saying.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Turn the volume up, or you won't hear what they're saying."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0936217d-66d2-5360-b88b-a30683720415" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Disjunctive Statements as Conditionals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38c9c24e-3b55-56ca-86a7-b2ce01e8f281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't apologize, then she won't forgive you." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422160} {:answer/id #uuid "719cf9b3-0ac6-54a8-b955-880ab8843c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you apologize, then she will forgive you." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422162} {:answer/id #uuid "8213100f-0dc7-5d4d-b8fd-6e029e273dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you apologize, then she won't forgive you." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422163} {:answer/id #uuid "a2794c58-1f77-5658-9986-016411aec9fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't apologize, then she will forgive you." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422164} {:answer/id #uuid "a2993367-1981-5e54-b3ef-99b9be03f44e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Apologize, and she will forgive you." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38c9c24e-3b55-56ca-86a7-b2ce01e8f281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't apologize, then she won't forgive you." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422160} :answer-field/id #uuid "e83d217a-41be-555a-a16a-343371ec8880" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4482556-1279-5932-bd2e-cbe957830200" :question/math-academy-id "q-182574" :question/problem "$\\,$ Either apologize, or she won't forgive you.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ Apologize.\n\n- $B:$ She won't forgive you.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't apologize.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you don't apologize, then she won't forgive you.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0936217d-66d2-5360-b88b-a30683720415" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Disjunctive Statements as Conditionals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d443744-cec7-50fa-b09c-8602760601c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you read the book, then you won't understand the lecture." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379262} {:answer/id #uuid "1c2fe104-86f6-5a71-b4ce-e3438ccc1c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't read the book, then you will understand the lecture." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379263} {:answer/id #uuid "805f4b30-a877-5ba5-9626-70177aa117fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't read the book, then you won't understand the lecture." :db/id #edb/ref 1759218637