 given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you get enough sleep, then you will be alert the next day.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ If you get enough sleep, you will be alert the next day.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eec6cf11-74cc-5b3a-b2fe-bd4285fe4a88" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Conditional Statement Using the Associative, Commutative, and Absorption Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "911a45b8-ba13-5065-bb06-c450effaabf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∨Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394326} {:answer/id #uuid "95480722-7569-5083-8b58-56b83301d195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394327} {:answer/id #uuid "8476685e-e104-531e-86f2-c09e6663f600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬X∨Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394328} {:answer/id #uuid "eee8dbbe-fc4d-5c20-9c21-6ae1d839c1fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394329} {:answer/id #uuid "77a3543d-dc9d-5f25-b6e9-2b5f550e3919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eee8dbbe-fc4d-5c20-9c21-6ae1d839c1fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394329} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6f0c2ff-63c9-5651-8370-33bd7ba743eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0467eb40-db5b-5041-af5a-7039ef1e5d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-81891" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬(X∧Y)⇒Y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(X∧Y)⇒Y≡¬(¬(X∧Y))∨Y.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(X∧Y))∨Y≡(X∧Y)∨Y.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the absorption law, we have\n\n$$\n(X∧Y)∨Y≡Y.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $Y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eec6cf11-74cc-5b3a-b2fe-bd4285fe4a88" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Conditional Statement Using the Associative, Commutative, and Absorption Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "191c421c-bbe9-5286-9beb-03027543bcc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B∨¬C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409566} {:answer/id #uuid "33e9615f-fd80-5202-949f-ebfef4f3e8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬B∨C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409569} {:answer/id #uuid "35dc661b-8042-5dd9-ae2c-1ddb0493316b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B∧¬C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409570} {:answer/id #uuid "442d26c2-04f3-57d7-adf7-53465469c631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬B∧C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409572} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2b2d55-0e4e-5769-9706-03ec9ecc763c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B∨C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "191c421c-bbe9-5286-9beb-03027543bcc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B∨¬C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409566} :answer-field/id #uuid "eea492f1-156d-5834-96c0-8454fc274282" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c7953127-b916-5d8d-98b4-eeece8a2e1ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-81892" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $(¬B∧C)⇒(B∨¬C).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n(¬B∧C)⇒(B∨¬C)≡¬(¬B∧C)∨(B∨¬C).\n$$\n\nWe can distribute the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law:\n\n$$\n¬(¬B∧C)∨(B∨¬C)≡(¬(¬B)∨¬C)∨(B∨¬C)\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n(¬(¬B)∨¬C)∨(B∨¬C)≡(B∨¬C)∨(B∨¬C).\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the disjunction of a statement with itself:\n\n$$\n(B∨¬C)∨(B∨¬C)≡B∨¬C\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $B∨¬C.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eec6cf11-74cc-5b3a-b2fe-bd4285fe4a88" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Conditional Statement Using the Associative, Commutative, and Absorption Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "173a22f9-03eb-5f52-84a2-64add79bb270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∧(Q∧R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422158} {:answer/id #uuid "2655898f-0ab3-55b9-b970-24883e6a1310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∨(Q∨R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422159} {:answer/id #uuid "666720f8-ee5e-5a0c-9eff-9b28566b29c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∧(Q∨¬R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422161} {:answer/id #uuid "be278a98-7931-527b-9249-5fe023ad7c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∧(Q∨R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422166} {:answer/id #uuid "d2702799-977f-5026-b7cd-3d37fe168a4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬P∧(Q∨R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be278a98-7931-527b-9249-5fe023ad7c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∧(Q∨R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422166} :answer-field/id #uuid "f19fca36-2b89-53ba-85fc-7765f2211e66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2bdcd5e4-a0e7-512a-97bb-f847c06388f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-82451" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬(P∧Q)⇒(P∧R).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(P∧Q)⇒(P∧R)≡¬(¬(P∧Q))∨(P∧R).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(P∧Q))∨(P∧R)≡(P∧Q)∨(P∧R).\n$$\n\nFinally, by the distributive laws, we have\n\n$$\n(P∧Q)∨(P∧R)≡P∧(Q∨R).\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $P∧(Q∨R).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eec6cf11-74cc-5b3a-b2fe-bd4285fe4a88" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Conditional Statement Using the Associative, Commutative, and Absorption Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3f58d25-2baf-5803-a04a-b27e820a661d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬A∨¬B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059149} {:answer/id #uuid "c66a1d54-c620-5f48-afe7-1e7ce769f22f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B∨A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059150} {:answer/id #uuid "d0723d53-fef2-5308-b4be-c8b87ad7abf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059151} {:answer/id #uuid "24bdfd5a-1f10-5997-ae73-32976d9d05cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬A∧B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059152} {:answer/id #uuid "41191d84-ae90-5573-8b9c-8105bade4385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A∧B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059153}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad38d3f-e396-5f47-8d88-1153012121a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65cec0f9-3a43-5683-99dc-e375aa81deb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-82455" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬(¬A∧B)⇒¬A$."}]}
